 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds a constant-time operation per Gaussian (covariance regularization and opacity scaling) during the rendering phase. It does not require additional training iterations, making it computationally efficient at test time compared to methods that require retraining or complex multi-resolution hierarchies.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory is required beyond the original Gaussian representation, as it is a training-free plugin.
-*   **Performance:** SA-GS performs comparably with or better than Mip-Splatting in terms of anti-aliasing quality across various settings and both bounded and unbounded scenes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Training-free, easy to integrate as a plugin, effective across different resolutions and camera distances.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the testing frequency parameter ($\sigma^2_{test}$) to match the scene scale and resolution.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds a constant-time filter application per Gaussian during rendering. Integration within pixels may increase per-pixel cost slightly compared to vanilla splatting, but avoids the overhead of multi-resolution pyramid construction during training [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory required for training data structures (e.g., mipmaps) as it is training-free [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Performs comparably with or better than Mip-Splatting in various settings and both bounded and unbounded scenes [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Training-free; can be applied to any pre-trained Gaussian splatting field as a plugin; explicitly aware of testing frequency [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires modification of the rendering pipeline to implement the adaptive filter and integration; does not address view-dependent effects as directly as methods like VDGS [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SA-GS was introduced as a training-free alternative to Mip-Splatting, which requires modifying the training procedure [S2, unofficial]. It addresses the issue of Gaussian scale mismatch between training and testing frequencies by applying 2D scale-adaptive filters at test time [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method is cited in recent surveys on 3D Gaussian Splatting advancements [S1].
+SA-GS was introduced to address the limitations of Mip-Splatting, which requires modifying the training procedure and uses 3D smoothing/2D Mip filters that are unaware of testing frequency [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the principle that observing Gaussians at different frequencies leads to mismatches between training and testing scales [S2, unofficial]. The method was proposed by Z. Song et al. [S2, unofficial].
 
